  "chamber_and_volume_logic": {
    "index": "The integral arithmetic orthogonal group is a finite-covolume lattice.  The reflection chamber is a fundamental domain for the full integral reflection subgroup, so infinite chamber volume is equivalent to infinite reflection index.",
    "infinite_volume": "The restriction of g to span(v0,d) is hyperbolic, with expanding eigenvalue >1 and positive translation length.  A ball of radius less than half that translation length about any chamber-interior point has pairwise disjoint g^n-translates, all contained in the g-invariant chamber.  Therefore its volume is infinite.",
    "logic_pass": true,
    "mirror_arrangement_invariant_under_g": "An integral isometry conjugates an integral reflection s_r to the integral reflection s_{g r}; hence it permutes all mirrors.",
    "same_chamber": "The regular endpoints v0 and gv0 have no separating mirror. Connected components of hyperbolic space minus the locally finite mirror union are exactly the open reflection chambers.",
    "stabilizer": "Because g permutes mirrors and maps v0 into the same component, g maps that component to itself."
  },
